#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184029 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "73571ff9-24a3-52b6-be57-3ef08652ebb7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First or Second Derivative of a Function at a Point Given Its Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "6e0ed74f-d678-5d0f-a4bf-3a284621f6d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-738" :question/problem "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of a function $f(x)$ about $x=2$ is given by\n\n$f(x)=1 + 4(x-2) - 12(x-2)^2$.\n\nWhat is the value of $f''(2)$?" :question/worked-solution "This is an expansion about the point $x=2$, so the coefficient of the second-degree term is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 2 f''(2)\n$$\n\nIn the given equation, we see that the coefficient of the second-degree term is $-12$, so we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 2 f''(2) = -12\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad f''(2) = -24\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184030 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fce4cd5a-537a-5eb4-a610-59d2bad95a89" :knowledge-point/title "Deducing the Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial Given the Graph of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "4073dad3-002e-5a92-85a8-d47682964069" :question/math-academy-id "e-739" :question/problem "The figure above shows the graph of a function $y = f(x)$. Which of the following could be the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=-1$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3825/Source/Images/e67c9dfab10704ddb8dfa9e9559f5e0d.png)\n\n1. $-1+2(x+1)-3(x+1)^2$\n2. $-1+4(x+1)+(x+1)^2$\n3. $-1-5(x+1)-2(x+1)^2$" :question/worked-solution "The second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=-1$ is\n\n$$\nP_2(x)=f(-1)+f'(-1)(x+1)+\\dfrac{f''(-1)}{2}(x+1)^2\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we observe:\n\n- $f(-1) <0$. Therefore, the first term of the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n- $f'(-1) >0$, because the slope of the tangent at $x=-1$ is positive. Therefore, the coefficient of the second term in the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n- $f''(-1) <0$, because the graph is concave down at $x=-1$. Therefore, the coefficient of the third term in the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\nOnly option I meets all of the above criteria."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184472 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fce4cd5a-537a-5eb4-a610-59d2bad95a89" :knowledge-point/title "Deducing the Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial Given the Graph of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8178110c-9240-5a77-bed6-92ad53268918" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 + (x + 3) - (x + 3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181281} {:answer/id #uuid "06c0de07-0d85-54c3-9898-e94dee930e3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 + x + x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181282} {:answer/id #uuid "55ec8771-be98-5a58-902d-830534f0fdef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 + x - x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181283} {:answer/id #uuid "5ac0b35d-2aff-508a-8c55-6b2b73a04ede" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2 + (x + 3) - (x + 3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181284} {:answer/id #uuid "76b61530-59da-58e7-af01-7c973356bd8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2 + (x + 3) + (x + 3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ac0b35d-2aff-508a-8c55-6b2b73a04ede" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2 + (x + 3) - (x + 3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181284} :answer-field/id #uuid "434a4c4f-1cc0-552f-bb4e-8dafda6e357e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181280}] :question/id #uuid "50192933-049d-5805-8ae1-413b4aad1117" :question/math-academy-id "q-15523" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3825/Source/Images/q-15523.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $f$. Which of the following could be the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f$ about $x =-3$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294399 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fce4cd5a-537a-5eb4-a610-59d2bad95a89" :knowledge-point/title "Deducing the Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial Given the Graph of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d5dac73-ec22-5f59-b6b4-5af467826122" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5+2(x-1)+6(x-1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294401} {:answer/id #uuid "51ef6dd2-41b9-5ce9-a150-e2e384cdbc9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5+2(x-1)-6(x-1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294402} {:answer/id #uuid "c7b707c4-8e0c-5a96-87b0-7024112b8190" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5-2(x-1)-6(x-1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294403} {:answer/id #uuid "5c4785b1-9940-5c81-bfb9-d993312c70d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5-2(x-1)-6(x-1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294404} {:answer/id #uuid "db86223e-0460-570a-80e8-13ae73d77628" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5-2(x-1)+6(x-1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294405}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db86223e-0460-570a-80e8-13ae73d77628" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5-2(x-1)+6(x-1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294405} :answer-field/id #uuid "c95bab29-2e85-5424-9339-70f5b63e00c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294400}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a4ea253-fa70-53c3-a318-c123823ef429" :question/math-academy-id "q-81063" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035241/assets/e483403ebe85920111334dacfc22dc907ab3c33cd38b33361d7634132032c811.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following could be the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f$ about $x=1?$" :question/worked-solution "The second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(1)+{f}^{′}(1)(x-1)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(1)}{2}(x-1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we observe:\n\n- $f(1)<0.$ Therefore, the first term of the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\n- ${f}^{′}(1)<0,$ because the slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is negative. Therefore, the coefficient of the second term in the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\n- ${f}^{″}(1)>0,$ because the graph is concave up at $x=1.$ Therefore, the coefficient of the third term in the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\nThe only option that meets the above criteria is\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=-5-2(x-1)+6(x-1)^{2}.\n$$"}]}
